[STEP 1] Live Socket Transfer Measurement & Transport Path Validation
1. SSH-Tunneled Path:
   Endpoint: 127.0.0.1:50051 (tunneled to 154.64.230.67:26514)
   Trials (1 MB): [13.1100s, 10.6480s, 8.6137s]
   Mean: 10.7906 s (0.0954 MB/s / 763.2 Kbps)

2. Direct Un-Tunneled NAT Path (Port Published at Creation):
   iperf3 to 154.64.230.67:26518:
     - Receiver Bitrate: 831,242 bps (0.831 Mbps / 0.104 MB/s)
     - Retransmissions: 257 TCP retransmits / 5s
     - Mean RTT: 181.89 ms
   Direct Raw Socket to 154.64.230.67:26576 (1 MB):
     - Trials: [10.873s, 15.074s, 15.701s]
     - Mean: 13.883 s (0.0720 MB/s)

Definitive Finding:
The ~0.1 MB/s throughput is confirmed NOT to be an SSH tunnel artifact.
It was measured identically across both an SSH tunnel and a direct, un-tunneled
TCP NAT port via iperf3 and raw Python socket (3 trials). The severe packet loss
(257 retransmits in 5s) is consistent with either host-level NAT/bridging overhead
or active traffic shaping / QoS policing between tenant containers on separate
chassis — we did not isolate which.

